    {"id":"evidence_mexico","scope":"mesoamerica_1450","citation":"Michael E. Smith, The Aztecs, 3rd ed. (Wiley-Blackwell, 2012), and Robert H. Barlow, The Extent of the Empire of the Culhua Mexica (1949).","note":"Supports the separate Triple Alliance partners, independent rivals, altepetl granularity, tributary practice, and the continuing war against Chalco in 1450."},
    {"id":"evidence_maya","scope":"maya_highlands_and_yucatan","citation":"Robert J. Sharer and Loa P. Traxler, The Ancient Maya, 6th ed. (Stanford University Press, 2006).","note":"Supports post-Mayapán political fragmentation and distinct Itza, Yucatec, K'iche', and Kaqchikel jurisdictions."},
    {"id":"evidence_caribbean","scope":"caribbean_chiefdoms","citation":"William F. Keegan and Corinne L. Hofman, The Caribbean before Columbus (Oxford University Press, 2017).","note":"Supports multiple Taíno chiefdom networks and autonomous Kalinago communities rather than later island-colony boundaries."},
    {"id":"evidence_northern_andes","scope":"muisca_tairona","citation":"Warwick Bray, Gold of El Dorado (Times Books, 1978), and Carl Henrik Langebaek, Regional Archaeology in the Muisca Territory (1995).","note":"Supports separate southern and northern Muisca confederations and autonomous Tairona chiefdoms."},
    {"id":"evidence_central_andes","scope":"tawantinsuyu_chimor_chincha","citation":"Terence N. D'Altroy, The Incas, 2nd ed. (Wiley-Blackwell, 2015), and María Rostworowski, History of the Inca Realm (Cambridge University Press, 1999).","note":"Supports early Pachacuti-era Tawantinsuyu, independent Chimor and Chincha, and regional Andean organization; it does not project later Inca expansion backward."},
    {"id":"evidence_amazon_east","scope":"amazonian_and_eastern_south_america","citation":"Michael Heckenberger and Eduardo Góes Neves, 'Amazonian Archaeology', Annual Review of Anthropology 38 (2009), and Julian H. Steward, ed., Handbook of South American Indians, volumes 1–3 (1946–1948).","note":"Supports populous Amazonian regional societies and decentralized Tupí-Guaraní peoples while avoiding fictitious centralized borders."},
    {"id":"evidence_southern_cone","scope":"southern_andes_and_lowlands","citation":"Julian H. Steward, ed., Handbook of South American Indians, volumes 2–3 (Smithsonian Institution, 1946–1948).","note":"Supports autonomous Mapuche, Diaguita-Calchaquí, Guaraní, Charrúa, and southern hunter-gatherer regions in the precolonial baseline."}
